#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = [
#     "httpx>=0.27",
#     "livekit>=1.1,<2",
#     "pyyaml>=6",
# ]
# ///
"""callvatest — agent-first CLI that regression-tests a deployed CallVA voice agent.

This tool is designed to be invoked by agents. Run `callvatest help` to load the
full usage contract on demand — do not rely on memorised knowledge of its surface.

It joins a real LiveKit room as a headless participant through the ordinary CallVA
WebRTC connect flow, drives the conversation over text, and then asserts what the
deployed agent actually did: the transcript it produced, the tools it called, the
arguments it generated, the results it answered from, and the fields the platform
stored after the call. There is no test-only runtime path — the agent under test
loads its production configuration and calls its production tools, so a passing
run is evidence about production behaviour.

Placing a call costs money and creates a real call record. `run`, `suite`, and
`probe` are therefore write verbs: a connection with "allow_write": false refuses
them, and no flag lifts that gate.

CONNECTIONS
  Configuration requires an explicit connections registry, even for one
  connection. Its values resolve by the cascade (first non-empty wins):
    1. flags          --api-url (non-secret only; a secret never has a flag)
    2. project env    .env.local then .env, walking up from $CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR (else cwd)
    3. user config    $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/callvatest/credentials.env
    4. process env    CALLVA_API_KEY / CALLVA_API_URL
  The key is only ever named, never passed on argv.
    CALLVA_API_KEY=<CallVA API key>        secret — the tenant that owns the agent
    CALLVA_API_URL=<base URL>              optional; default
                                           https://api.callva.one/api/v1

  A project that tests several CallVA tenants declares them in
  <project>/capabilities/callvatest/connections.json:
    {
      "default": "<id>",
      "connections": {
        "<id>": {
          "api_url": "https://api.example.com/api/v1",
          "secret_env": "CALLVA_<ID>_API_KEY",
          "allow_write": true
        }
      }
    }
  "secret_env" names the environment variable holding the key; naming a variable
  the project already defines keeps one home for the secret. "allow_write": false
  makes a connection observation-only — useful for pointing at a live tenant you
  must not place calls into.

  Selection: --connection <id>. Unflagged, the registry's "default" pointer
  applies, else the sole entry; several entries without a pointer is refused.

JUDGES
  A `semantic` assertion is settled by a judge model, declared in a "judges"
  block of the same connections file so one file holds every credential this
  harness resolves:

    "judges": {
      "default": "haiku",
      "connections": {
        "haiku":   {"provider": "anthropic",
                    "model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
                    "secret_env": "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"},
        "gpt-oss": {"provider": "cerebras", "model": "gpt-oss-120b",
                    "secret_env": "CEREBRAS_API_KEY"}
      }
    }

  Providers are "anthropic" and "cerebras"; the model is named per judge, so a
  backend is switched in the registry or per invocation with --judge <id>,
  never in a scenario. Keys resolve by the same cascade as the API key and are
  never passed on argv. The judge is asked at temperature 0 for a JSON verdict,
  and it is resolved only when a scenario actually declares a `semantic` claim.

  A judge runs after the call has ended, so its accuracy decides the choice and
  its latency does not.

SCENARIOS
  Project-owned CallVA artefacts live under `callva/` in the project, by kind.
  Scenarios are one kind:

    <project>/callva/tests/<agent>/<language>/<scenario>.yaml

  `run` and `suite` walk up from the working directory to find `callva/tests`.
  --path overrides the search entirely.

  A scenario is YAML:

    id: earliest-slot
    agent: <agent-label>          # ids label, or an agent uuid
    language: en
    turns:
      - say: "I would like to book an appointment."
        expect:
          tools_not_called: ["Availability Lookup"]
          reply_not_matches: "(?i)cannot help"
      - say: "Tell me the earliest time you have."
        expect:
          tool_called: "Availability Lookup"
          tool_args:
            service_id: "<service-id>"
          semantic: "The agent offers the earliest slot from the result."
    expect:                       # evaluated once the call has settled
      audio_observed: true
      assistant_turn_after_tool: true
      no_internal_ids: true
      tool_call_count:
        "Availability Lookup": 1
      post_call:
        status: completed

  A tool is named by a case-insensitive substring of its automation name, or by
  an automation uuid. Times, dates, and slots are never pinned as literals — they
  are asserted against the tool result the agent actually received.

  `post_call` reads the call record the platform stored once the call settled:
  its own outcome fields, and any custom field the tenant defines on a call.
  Each named field is compared as a string, so a scenario asserts the tenant's
  own schema without this harness knowing it.

  `semantic` states one claim in prose and hands it to a judge model together
  with the tool results of that turn and what the agent said; a call-level
  `semantic` is given the whole transcript and every run. Use it where the
  medium defeats matching in code — an agent speaks "kell kümme", never 10:00,
  and no regular expression will call that the same time. Everything structured
  (which tool ran, its arguments, counts, leaked ids) stays deterministic: code
  is exact, free, and never flickers. A judge that cannot answer fails the
  check; it never passes it.

COMMANDS
  probe [--agent <id|label>] [--say TEXT ...] [--turns N]
        One diagnostic call with no assertions: connect, join, drive the turns
        given by --say, then print everything observed — transcript with
        timings, agent state changes, audio frames, the resolved call id, tool
        runs with their arguments, and tool results. This is the first thing to
        run against a new agent, and the first thing to run when a scenario
        fails for reasons the report does not explain.

  run <scenario> [--agent <id|label>] [--report FILE]
        Execute one scenario and print its verdict. Exit 1 when it fails.

  suite [<path>] [--agent <id|label>] [--report FILE]
        Execute every scenario under a path, sequentially. Exit 1 if any fails.

  fixtures [<path>] [--judge <id>] [--all-judges] [--report FILE]
        Judge the judge, without placing a call. Each fixture case freezes tool
        results, what an agent said, a claim, and the verdict a competent judge
        must reach; the run reports every disagreement. Exit 1 when any case
        disagrees. Fixtures live in <project>/callva/judge/:

          id: et-times
          cases:
            - claim: "Every time the agent speaks appears in the tool result."
              results: ['{"earliest": "reede, 4. september 09:00"}']
              said: "Kõige varasem vaba aeg on reedel kell üheksa."
              expect: true

        Half the cases must expect false, or the run cannot tell a judge that
        reads from one that agrees with everything. --all-judges runs the whole
        set past every declared judge, which is how backends are compared on
        material that matters rather than on a benchmark.

        `known_gap: true` marks a case every judge already fails — a measured
        limit rather than a regression. It still runs and is still reported, but
        it does not fail the run, because a set that is permanently red gates
        nothing; the day a judge starts getting it right is reported too.

  doctor
        Per connection: prove the key resolves and reaches the API, and that the
        LiveKit RTC stack imports. Names the agents the key can see, and asks
        each declared judge to settle a trivial claim.

  Contract verbs: help, doctor, connections, stub, manifest --json,
  guide [<topic>], ids list|get|set|rm, refs.

GLOBAL OPTIONS
  --connection <id>   select a registry connection
  --judge <id>        select a judge for this run's semantic assertions
  --key-env <KEY>     resolve the API key from a different env/cred KEY
  --api-url <URL>     override the base URL for this invocation
  --json              machine-readable output where a verb has a prose default
  --timeout <S>       per-turn timeout in seconds (default 45)
  --budget <S>        whole-call budget in seconds (default 300)

EXIT CODES
  0 ok · 1 assertion failed · 2 credentials · 3 not found · 4 policy
  5 upstream/transport · 6 invalid input · 7 timeout waiting on the agent
"""

